<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-review-problems-3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Review Problems #3</title>

<introduction>
<p>
These are problems from the Chapter 4 topics that were not assigned in
Assignment 7, collected here for review. They are not assigned, but they are
fair game on an exam. If you would like to review the material first, see
<xref ref="sec-skel-fmv-functions" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="q1-1">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function
<md>
h(x,y,z) = z + \sqrt{x^2+y^2} + 1 .
</md>
Sketch the level surface of <m>h</m> that passes through the point
<m>(3,4,-5)</m>. Clearly label the value of <m>h</m> on that surface,
and label the coordinate axes.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The level surfaces of <m>h</m> are the surfaces <m>h(x,y,z) = c</m>.
To find which one passes through <m>(3,4,-5)</m> we simply evaluate:
<md>
<mrow>h(3,4,-5) \amp = -5 + \sqrt{3^2+4^2} + 1</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -5 + \sqrt{25} + 1</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -5 + 5 + 1 = 1 .</mrow>
</md>
So the level surface we want is <m>h(x,y,z) = 1</m>.
</p>

<p>
Now we simplify the equation of that level surface:
<md>
<mrow>z + \sqrt{x^2+y^2} + 1 \amp = 1</mrow>
<mrow>z + \sqrt{x^2+y^2} \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>z \amp = -\sqrt{x^2+y^2} .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
This is a cone. Indeed, squaring gives <m>z^2 = x^2+y^2</m>, which is
the full double cone; but the equation <m>z = -\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>
forces <m>z \le 0</m>, so we keep only the <em>lower</em> nappe. The
vertex is at the origin, and the horizontal trace at height
<m>z = -k</m> (with <m>k \gt 0</m>) is the circle
<m>x^2+y^2 = k^2</m> of radius <m>k</m>. In other words the cone opens
downwards, and the slope of every straight line drawn on it from the
vertex is <m>-1</m>. The point <m>(3,4,-5)</m> lies on it, since
<m>-\sqrt{3^2+4^2} = -5</m>. See <xref ref="fig-q1-cone"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-q1-cone">
<caption>
The level surface <m>h(x,y,z) = 1</m>, that is the downward cone
<m>z = -\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>. It passes through <m>(3,4,-5)</m>,
which lies on the circle <m>x^2+y^2=25</m> in the plane <m>z=-5</m>.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<description>
<p>
A cone with its vertex at the origin opening downwards around the
negative z axis. The horizontal circle of radius five is drawn on it in
the plane z equals negative five, and the point three, four, negative
five is marked on that circle.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=11cm, height=9.5cm,
  view={-35}{18},
  xmin=-6.5, xmax=6.5, ymin=-6.5, ymax=6.5, zmin=-6.5, zmax=2.2,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  xtick={-5,0,5}, ytick={-5,0,5}, ztick={-5,0},
  clip=false,
]
% the cone z = -sqrt(x^2+y^2), drawn in polar form
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=mblue, draw=mblue,
          fill opacity=0.30, draw opacity=0.35,
          domain=0:5.6, y domain=0:360, samples=12, samples y=41,
          z buffer=sort]
  ({x*cos(y)},{x*sin(y)},{-x});
% the trace circle at z = -5
\addplot3[very thick, mverm, domain=0:360, samples=60]
  ({5*cos(x)},{5*sin(x)},{-5});
% the marked point (3,4,-5)
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2pt, mred] coordinates {(3,4,-5)};
\node[mred, anchor=west, fill=white, fill opacity=0.75, text opacity=1,
      inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:3,4,-5) {$(3,4,-5)$};
\node[mverm, anchor=north] at (axis cs:0,-5.6,-5.6)
  {$x^2+y^2=25$};
\node[mblue, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:0.72,0.86)
  {$h=1$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q2-1">
<statement>
<p>
Sketch three level curves of
<md>
h(x,y) = \frac{y^2}{x} ,
</md>
including the one that passes through the point <m>(-1,2)</m>. Clearly
label the value of <m>h</m> on each curve.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
First we find the level through <m>(-1,2)</m>:
<md>
h(-1,2) = \frac{2^2}{-1} = -4 .
</md>
So one of the three curves must be <m>h(x,y) = -4</m>.
</p>

<p>
In general the level curve <m>h(x,y) = c</m> is
<md>
\frac{y^2}{x} = c \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad y^2 = c\,x
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad x = \frac{y^2}{c}
\qquad (c \ne 0) .
</md>
Each of these is a <em>parabola opening along the</em> <m>x</m><em>-axis</em>,
with vertex at the origin. If <m>c \gt 0</m> it opens to the right, and
if <m>c \lt 0</m> it opens to the left. Note that the domain of
<m>h</m> excludes <m>x = 0</m>, so the vertex itself, the origin, must
be removed from every level curve.
</p>

<p>
Let us take the three levels <m>c = -4</m>, <m>c = 1</m> and
<m>c = 4</m>:
<md>
<mrow>h \amp = -4: \amp\qquad x \amp = -\frac{y^2}{4}
  \amp\amp \text{(opens left)},</mrow>
<mrow>h \amp = 1: \amp\qquad x \amp = y^2
  \amp\amp \text{(opens right, narrow)},</mrow>
<mrow>h \amp = 4: \amp\qquad x \amp = \frac{y^2}{4}
  \amp\amp \text{(opens right, wide)}.</mrow>
</md>
The larger <m>|c|</m> is, the wider the parabola. These are drawn in
<xref ref="fig-q2-levels"/>.
</p>

<p>
One more remark: the level <m>c = 0</m> is special. There
<m>y^2 = 0</m>, so the level curve <m>h = 0</m> is not a parabola at
all but the <m>x</m>-axis with the origin removed.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-q2-levels">
<caption>
Three level curves of <m>h(x,y) = y^2/x</m>. The curve through
<m>(-1,2)</m> is the level <m>h = -4</m>, which opens to the left. The
origin is excluded from every level curve, since <m>h</m> is undefined
when <m>x = 0</m>.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<description>
<p>
Three parabolas with vertices at the origin and axes along the x axis.
One opens to the left and is labelled h equals negative four; two open
to the right and are labelled h equals one and h equals four. The point
negative one comma two is marked on the leftward parabola.
</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-q2-levels">
<diagram dimensions="(430,380)" margins="30">
<definition>cm4(t) = (-t^2/4, t)</definition>
<definition>c1(t) = (t^2, t)</definition>
<definition>c4(t) = (t^2/4, t)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-4.6,-3.4,4.6,3.9)">
<grid-axes at="q2l-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="q2l-m4" function="cm4" domain="(-3.2,3.2)"
                  stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="q2l-1" function="c1" domain="(-2.0,2.0)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="q2l-4" function="c4" domain="(-3.2,3.2)"
                  stroke="#009E73" thickness="2.5"/>
<point at="q2l-P" p="(-1,2)" fill="#8B1A4A"/>
<label at="q2l-Plab" anchor="(-1.20,2.18)" alignment="nw" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>(-1,2)</m>
</label>
<label at="q2l-m4lab" anchor="(-2.9,3.45)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>h=-4</m>
</label>
<label at="q2l-1lab" anchor="(4.45,1.60)" alignment="nw" color="#0072B2">
<m>h=1</m>
</label>
<label at="q2l-4lab" anchor="(3.35,3.45)" alignment="w" color="#009E73">
<m>h=4</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Three parabolas sharing the origin as vertex and the x axis as axis of symmetry. An orange one opens to the left, labelled h equals negative four. A blue narrow one and a green wide one open to the right, labelled h equals one and h equals four. A dark red dot marks the point negative one comma two on the orange curve.">
  <annotation ref="q2l-m4" text="The level curve h equals negative four, the parabola x equals minus y squared over four, opening to the left."/>
  <annotation ref="q2l-1" text="The level curve h equals one, the parabola x equals y squared, opening to the right."/>
  <annotation ref="q2l-4" text="The level curve h equals four, the parabola x equals y squared over four, opening to the right and wider than the previous one."/>
  <annotation ref="q2l-P" text="The point negative one comma two, which lies on the level curve h equals negative four."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

